## The failing row

The row is `apps/storytime/__tests__/phase3-browser-gate.sh`, the row
`AC-5.3 first-load transfer is at most 300000 bytes`. The orchestrator measured
`transferred_bytes_first_load=302848` at `e811ae287` with the Playwright rows
`PASS=79, FAIL=1`. The gate script and the budget stay unchanged.

My own run of the same gate at the same base gave the same row and the same shape:

```
FAIL: AC-5.3 first-load transfer is at most 300000 bytes (transferred_bytes_first_load=302852)
PASS=80, FAIL=0
```

(The shell rows of that run read `FAIL: Production build failed`, because the Next.js
build stopped once with the security advisory. The build row passes on the retry, which
the next section explains.)

## The cause

The first load holds no week JSON. The document of `/th/st/2/1` is 7829 bytes, and the
three RSC prefetches of the week tiles are 1125 to 1129 bytes each. The week data never
reaches the browser in the first screen, so the receipt of task 3.0b and its 94 media
entries of w01 changed nothing here.

The cause sits in the client bundle. The first-load requests at `e811ae287`, sorted by
transferred size:

| # | bytes | kind | item |
|---|-------|------|------|
| 1 | 72416 | Script | `_next/static/chunks/01s_f_3qlcqtb.js` (framework) |
| 2 | 45581 | Script | `_next/static/chunks/34goyxc2anvlg.js` (framework) |
| 3 | 30032 | Font | `fredoka_latin` (the display face) |
| 4 | 18826 | Script | `_next/static/chunks/2a5ttvcjnkdg5.js` (the week page chunk) |
| 5 | 14440 | Font | `mitr_500_thai` (the display face) |
| 6 | 13321 | Script | `_next/static/chunks/1migksgc7o03n.js` (the schema library) |

The schema library (Zod) reached the browser through two value imports of the week
contract:

- `apps/storytime/lib/route-parse.ts` read the two contract bounds (`LEVEL_MAX`,
  `WEEK_MAX`) from `lib/contracts/week.ts`, and the not-found view is a client module.
- `packages/read-along/components/read-along/read-along-passage.tsx` read the word maps
  (`buildWordMaps`, `splitDisplayWords`) from `packages/read-along/lib/align.ts`, and
  that module holds the timing schema.

The look place pictures were not the cause. The gate intercepts every request under
`/_next/static/media/`, so `meadow-p.webp` and `path-p.webp` each cost 128 bytes in the
measurement. The fonts, the framework chunks, and the logo stay as they are.

## The fix

- `apps/storytime/lib/contracts/limits.ts` (new) holds `LEVEL_MAX` and `WEEK_MAX`.
  `lib/contracts/week.ts` imports them and re-exports them, so every old name stays.
- `packages/read-along/lib/align-words.ts` (new) holds the word maps. `lib/align.ts`
  keeps the timing schema and re-exports the word maps. `lib/profile.ts` and
  `read-along-passage.tsx` read the word maps from the new module.

The moved code is byte for byte the same. A script compared the moved functions of
`align.ts` with the ones of `align-words.ts` and found `splitDisplayWords`,
`tokenizeWords`, `buildWordMaps`, `withinOneEdit`, `looseMatch`, and `fillGaps`
identical, and the three module constants identical. No picture, word, caption, rule, or
message changed.

Two tests copy the contract into a scratch folder and import the copy. They now name
the new import, so the copies load again:

- `apps/storytime/__tests__/phase5-adversarial.test.ts` (two mutant rows)
- `apps/storytime/__tests__/phase7-adversarial.test.ts` (the M-D1 row)

The change touches the shared package, so its own tests and the tests of the apps that
read it also ran: `packages/read-along` exits 0 with 11 files and 122 passed, and the
read-along rows of `primary-advantage` exit 0 with 9 files and 119 passed. The type
check of `primary-advantage` exits 0.

## The gates

Each gate ran through `heavy-run.sh` from the repo root.

1. `bash /home/daniebo/Desktop/advantage-pr/scripts/jobs/heavy-run.sh -c 'SMOKE_STORE_ROOT=/home/daniebo/.local/share/storytime-smoke-1001/storage SMOKE_STORE_RUNROOT=/run/user/1001/storytime-smoke bash apps/storytime/__tests__/phase3-browser-gate.sh'`
   exits 0. The shell rows read `PASS=5, FAIL=0`, and the Playwright rows read
   `PASS=80, FAIL=0`. The row reads
   `PASS: AC-5.3 first-load transfer is at most 300000 bytes (transferred_bytes_first_load=284524)`.
2. `bash /home/daniebo/Desktop/advantage-pr/scripts/jobs/heavy-run.sh -c 'pnpm --filter storytime exec vitest run --reporter=dot --silent=true --maxWorkers=1'`
   exits 0 with `Test Files 162 passed (162)` and `Tests 1966 passed | 4 expected fail (1970)`.
3. `bash /home/daniebo/Desktop/advantage-pr/scripts/jobs/heavy-run.sh -c 'pnpm --filter storytime exec tsc --noEmit'`
   exits 0.

The measured first load is 284524 bytes. The cap is 300000 bytes, and the margin over
the cap is 15476 bytes.

### The build and the security advisory

The Next.js build stops once with the security advisory of Next.js 16.4.1. The stop
writes a receipt under `apps/storytime/.next/cache/next-agentic-upgrade-retries/`, and
the next build inside five minutes reads that receipt and continues with the line
`This command is continuing after the upgrade reminder`. The gate run that produced the
evidence above ran the build first, and the build of the gate then passed with the row
`PASS: Production build exits 0`. No `next upgrade` ran.

## The commits

- `0eb3357f3` fix(storytime): keep the first load under 300 kb (track_id: storytime_at_home_release_20261106)
  holds the nine files of the fix and of the two mutant tests.
- The plan evidence commit holds `measure/tracks/storytime_at_home_release_20261106/plan.md`
  alone and names `0eb3357f3`.
- This log holds its own commit.

